# Fathom — Game state

This file defines the observable state the game holds: every field it carries, the
type of that field, and what it means. The debugging surface's readings project
exactly this state, so it is what a scenario poses and reads back.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build owns the shape of the state entirely. How it is organized, how the maze
and the fog are held, and which values are stored rather than derived are all
yours to decide. What is fixed is that the state carries the facts below, under
the names and meanings given, and returns to its title-screen values when the
debugging surface's `reset` is called.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The state is a value the engine holds and every frame replaces, so a frame builds
the next state from the current one rather than writing into it, and every reader
is handed it as `DeepReadonly<FathomState>`. `src/game.ts` declares and exports
that type. How the state is organized inside `FathomState`, how the maze and the
fog are held, and which values are stored rather than derived are yours to decide.
What is fixed is that the state carries the facts below, under the names and
meanings given, that every value the game carries from one frame to the next lives
in it rather than in a module-level variable or a closure, and that it returns to
its title-screen values when the debugging surface's `reset` is called.
{{else}}
The state lives in the framework objects the engine owns: the game instance, the
world that is open, that world's game mode and game state, and the actors in it.
Fathom runs in one world for the whole session, so the game definition registers a
single level, the game never opens another, and every screen is a value of
`screen`. How Fathom maps onto those objects, how the maze and the fog are held,
and which values are stored rather than derived are yours to decide, within the
framework's own line: nothing lives in a module-level variable or a closure
outside them. What is fixed is that the state carries the facts below, under the
names and meanings given, and returns to its title-screen values when the
debugging surface's `reset` is called.
{{/if}}

## The snapshot

The surface's `snapshot` returns a plain, JSON-serializable object carrying every
field below. It is a pure read that changes nothing.

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "countdown" | "playing" | "paused" | "cleared" | "gameover",
  menuIndex: <number | null>,    // the highlighted item on the current menu
  titleIndex: <number>,          // the title menu's remembered selection
  depth: <number>,               // the current maze's depth, from 1
  score: <number>,
  lives: <number>,
  muted: <boolean>,              // whether the mute toggle is on
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
  autoStep: <boolean>,           // whether the frame loop advances the simulation
{{/if}}
  planktonRemaining: <number>,   // plankton left in the current maze
  drifterIn: <number>,           // seconds until the cadence admits the next drifter
  brightness: <number>,          // G, in [0, 1]
  brightHold: <number>,          // seconds left on the brightness hold
  visionRadius: <number>,        // V, the line-of-sight light radius, in logical units
{{#if (eq variant.slug "kindle")}}
  windowRadius: <number>,        // R, the outer vision-circle radius, in logical units
{{/if}}
  sonar: { ready: <boolean>, cooldown: <number>, range: <number> },
  ink: { ready: <boolean>, cooldown: <number> },

  grid: {
    cols: <number>,    // GRID_COLS
    rows: <number>,    // GRID_ROWS
    tile: <number>,    // TILE
    originX: <number>, // GRID_ORIGIN_X, column 0's left edge
    originY: <number>, // GRID_ORIGIN_Y, row 0's top edge
  },

  tiles: [ <string>, ... ],      // GRID_ROWS strings of GRID_COLS characters
  plankton: [ <string>, ... ],   // the same layout, one plankton character per tile
  visibility: [ <string>, ... ], // the same layout, one visibility character per tile

  forager: {
    x: <number>, y: <number>,    // its center
    tx: <number>, ty: <number>,  // the tile it is on
    dir: "up" | "down" | "left" | "right",
    moving: <boolean>,
  },

  drifters: [
    {
      x: <number>, y: <number>,
      tx: <number>, ty: <number>,
      lit: <boolean>,
      mind: <boolean>,           // whether its own mind is running
      travel: <boolean>,         // whether its body travels
    },
  ],

  predators: [
    {
      kind: "lanternjaw" | "gloamfin" | "flarefish",
      x: <number>, y: <number>,
      tx: <number>, ty: <number>,
      dir: "up" | "down" | "left" | "right",
      state: "den" | "wander" | "chase" | "search",
      released: <boolean>,
      mind: <boolean>,           // whether its own mind is running
      travel: <boolean>,         // whether its body travels
      speed: <number>,           // the rate it travels at in its current state
      alert: <boolean>,
      lit: <boolean>,
      detectRange: <number | null>,   // Lanternjaw and Flarefish
      hearingRange: <number | null>,  // Gloamfin
      hearingLock: <boolean | null>,  // Gloamfin
      flareCharging: <boolean | null>, // Flarefish
      flaring: <boolean | null>,       // Flarefish
      flareRadius: <number | null>,    // Flarefish
    },
  ],

  pulses: [
    {
      source: "forager" | "gloamfin",
      tint: "cyan" | "violet" | "orange",
      ox: <number>, oy: <number>,  // the tile the pulse originated from
      front: <number>,             // how far the front has traveled, in tiles
      range: <number>,             // its maximum path range, in tiles
    },
  ],

  inkClouds: [
    { x: <number>, y: <number>, radius: <number>, remaining: <number> },
  ],

  simTime: <number>,   // accumulated simulation time, in seconds
}
```

## The screens

`screen` is the top-level state machine, and it names the screen the game is
showing. Every build carries all seven, and `specs/ui.md` defines what each one
shows and how the game moves between them.

| `screen` | The game is |
| --- | --- |
| `"title"` | On the title screen and its menu. |
| `"howto"` | On the how-to-play screen. |
| `"countdown"` | Holding at the top of a maze, before control resumes. |
| `"playing"` | In live play. |
| `"paused"` | Paused over a frozen maze. |
| `"cleared"` | On the interstitial after a maze is cleared. |
| `"gameover"` | On the game-over screen after the last life is lost. |

## The menus

- `menuIndex` is the index of the highlighted item on the menu the current
  screen shows, counted from `0` over the items `specs/ui.md` lists for that
  menu. It is `null` on `"howto"`, `"countdown"`, `"playing"` and `"cleared"`,
  which show no menu.
- `titleIndex` is the title menu's remembered selection: the index of the item
  last confirmed on that menu, `0` before any of them has been. It is never
  `null`, and `specs/ui.md` states what sets it and which arrivals at `"title"`
  take `menuIndex` from it.

## The run

- `depth` is the current maze's depth, a whole number from `1`. It fixes the
  predator roster and the sonar range, as `specs/progression.md` and
  `specs/predators.md` state.
- `score` is the running score for the whole run, across every maze of it.
- `lives` is the lives remaining, `START_LIVES` (`3`) at the start of a dive. The
  life being played is not counted among them, so the game ends when contact costs
  a life and `lives` is already `0`.
- `muted` is the game's readable copy of the runtime's mute bit, refreshed every
  frame. The runtime owns muting.
- `simTime` is accumulated simulation time in seconds. Every tick adds its own
  length, whatever the screen, the menus and the paused screen included.
- `planktonRemaining` is how many plankton are left in the current maze.

## The forager's senses

- `brightness` is `G`, in `[0, 1]`, the forager's brightness as
  `specs/sensing.md` defines it.
- `brightHold` is the seconds left on the `BRIGHT_HOLD` (`1.0 s`) hold eating a
  plankton arms, `0` once it has expired. `G` is steady while it runs and decays
  on the `specs/sensing.md` curve from the moment it reaches `0`.
- `visionRadius` is `V`, the radius of the line-of-sight light pocket, in logical
  units. It is derived from `brightness` by the formula in `specs/sensing.md`
  rather than held independently.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "kindle")}}
- `windowRadius` is `R`, the radius of the outer vision circle the forager
  carries, in logical units. It is derived from `brightness` by the formula in
  `specs/sensing.md` rather than held independently.
{{/if}}
- `sonar.cooldown` is the seconds left until the sonar pulse is ready and
  `sonar.ready` is `true` exactly when that is `0`. `sonar.range` is `E`, the
  pulse's maximum path range in tiles at the current depth.
- `ink.cooldown` is the seconds left until ink is ready and `ink.ready` is `true`
  exactly when that is `0`.

## The maze

`grid` is the tile grid's frame, and it reports the fixed geometry
`specs/overview.md` states: `GRID_COLS` (`36`) columns and `GRID_ROWS` (`18`) rows
of `TILE` (`32`) logical units each, with column `0`'s left edge at
`GRID_ORIGIN_X` (`64`) and row `0`'s top edge at `GRID_ORIGIN_Y` (`80`). The
center of tile `(tx, ty)` is therefore at
`(GRID_ORIGIN_X + tx * TILE + TILE / 2, GRID_ORIGIN_Y + ty * TILE + TILE / 2)`.

`tiles` is the layout, one string per row from the top, one character per column
from the left.

| Character | The tile is |
| --- | --- |
| `#` | Rock. |
| `.` | Open corridor. |
| `g` | The den gate. |
| `d` | Den interior. |

`plankton` is where the plankton stand this instant, in the same layout.

| Character | The tile |
| --- | --- |
| `*` | Holds a plankton. |
| `-` | Holds none. |

`planktonRemaining` is how many `*` the layer carries. Rock, the den gate and the
den interior hold none, so those tiles are always `-`.

`visibility` is the per-tile visibility this instant, in the same layout.

| Character | The tile is |
| --- | --- |
| `u` | Unrevealed, never touched by light, sonar or a flare. |
| `r` | Remembered, revealed earlier and not lit now. |
| `l` | Lit right now. |

Visibility is what the fog of war has revealed, as `specs/sensing.md` defines
it.{{#if (eq variant.slug "kindle")}} The vision circle is a rendering mask over that and reveals nothing, so a
remembered tile outside the circle still reports `r`.{{/if}}

## The forager

- `x` and `y` are the forager's center, in logical units.
- `tx` and `ty` are the tile it is on, the tile whose bounds contain its center.
- `dir` is its facing, always one of the four cardinal directions, whether it is
  moving or at rest.
- `moving` is `true` while it is traveling and `false` while it is at rest, which
  is a different question from `dir`.

## The drifters

`drifters` lists every bonus drifter in the maze. A drifter that has been eaten
leaves the list.

- `x` and `y` are its center, in logical units, and `tx` and `ty` are the tile it
  is on, the tile whose bounds contain its center.
- `lit` is `true` while its body is being drawn this instant, by the forager's
  light or by a flare. Its amber mote is a separate drawing, shown under the
  amber-light rule `specs/sensing.md` fixes, so `lit` answers for the body alone.
- `mind` is `true` while it decides its own wander.
- `travel` is `true` while its body carries those decisions through the maze.

`drifterIn` is the seconds left on the bonus-drifter cadence: the countdown that
admits the next drifter at the den gate, as `specs/gameplay.md` fixes it. It is
`DRIFTER_INTERVAL` (`25 s`) at the top of a maze and again from each admission,
and it never exceeds that. What it does while the maze already holds
`DRIFTER_MAX` of them is the cadence's own business, because such a maze admits
none whatever the countdown reads.

## The predators

`predators` lists every predator of the current roster, **in release order**: the
order `specs/predators.md` fixes for the den, which is the order the roster adds
them in. A predator the debugging surface adds goes at the end of the list. The
list therefore has one stable index per predator for as long as the roster stands,
and the debugging surface's predator operations select a predator by that index.

- `kind` is which hunter it is.
- `x` and `y` are its center, in logical units, and `tx` and `ty` the tile it is
  on.
- `dir` is its facing, always one of the four cardinal directions.
- `speed` is the rate it travels at in its current `state`, in logical units
  per second.
- `mind` is `true` while it senses and decides for itself.
- `travel` is `true` while its body carries what its mind decides through the
  maze.
- `alert` is `true` only while its detection alert is firing, which is the
  `ALERT_TIME` (`0.5 s`) window after a Gloamfin or a Flarefish acquires a fix.
  It is always `false` for the Lanternjaw, which fires no alert.
- `lit` is `true` while its body is being drawn this instant, whether by the
  forager's light, a sonar mark, a flare, or its own alert.
- `detectRange` is the light detection range the Lanternjaw and the Flarefish have
  this instant, in logical units, and `null` for the Gloamfin.
- `hearingRange` is how far the Gloamfin's close-range hearing reaches, in logical
  units, and `null` for the others.
- `hearingLock` is `true` while the Gloamfin holds a continuous close-range
  hearing lock on the forager, and `null` for the others.
- `flareCharging` is `true` while the Flarefish's pre-bloom charge-up glow is
  showing, `flaring` is `true` while its bloom is burning, and `flareRadius` is
  the bloom's current lit radius in logical units, `0` when it is not flaring. All
  three are `null` for the others.

### `state` and `released`

`state` and `released` answer two different questions about a predator, and
neither stands in for the other.

`state` says where it is and what it is doing. `"den"` means the predator is in
the den chamber, which includes one whose turn has come and which is still
swimming across the chamber toward the gate; it stops being `"den"` when the
predator is out. `"wander"` is patrolling with no fix, `"chase"` is pursuing a
fix, and `"search"` is the Gloamfin's cast-about at a tile the forager has left.

`released` says whether its turn has come. It is `false` while a predator waits
its slot in the staggered schedule `specs/predators.md` fixes, and `true` from the
moment that slot arrives, before it has crossed the gate and whatever `state`
says.

## The wavefronts and the ink

`pulses` lists every sonar wavefront in flight, the forager's own pulses and the
Gloamfin's pings alike.

- `source` is what cast it, and `tint` its color: `"cyan"` for the forager's
  pulse, `"violet"` for an ordinary Gloamfin ping, and `"orange"` for the
  Gloamfin's guaranteed lost-you ping.
- `ox` and `oy` are the tile it originated from.
- `front` is how far the leading edge has traveled from that tile, in corridor
  steps, and `range` is the furthest it will travel, in the same steps. A pulse
  leaves the list once its front passes its range.

`inkClouds` lists every ink cloud still standing, each fixed at the center `x`,
`y` it was released at, with its `radius` in logical units and `remaining`, the
seconds of life it has left. A cloud leaves the list when `remaining` reaches `0`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
## The driven flag

`autoStep` is `true` while the frame loop advances the simulation from the wall
clock, which is how a build starts and how it is played. Turning it off leaves the
game rendering every frame and advancing only when the debugging surface says so.

{{/if}}
## The contract

- Every field above is present from the moment the game has initialized, and none
  of them is optional. A field whose kind does not carry it reports `null`, as the
  predator fields state, rather than going missing.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
- The state handed to `update`, to `render`, to every diagnostic source and to
  every operation of the debugging surface is read-only, as
  `DeepReadonly<FathomState>`. `update` and the surface's poses return a new
  `FathomState` built from the one they received; `render`, the sources and the
  surface's readings return no state.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "structured-2d")}}
- The state is written only through the paths play runs on: the player controller
  resolves the frame's input into it, the game mode's tick advances it, and the
  debugging surface's poses arrange it at the call. Rendering reads it and writes
  nothing.
{{/if}}
- Anything the build keeps beyond the fields above is refreshed by every
  operation that could invalidate it, so a single-field pose leaves the game
  consistent.
- A field above that is a function of the world as it stands — a body's `tx` and
  `ty`, `visionRadius`{{#if (eq variant.slug "kindle")}} and `windowRadius`{{/if}},
  `sonar.range`, `sonar.ready`, `ink.ready`, `planktonRemaining`, and a
  predator's `speed`, `detectRange` and `hearingRange` — may be worked out at the
  read or kept as a stored copy, whichever the build prefers, and the debugging
  surface's `reconcile` is what brings a stored copy back into agreement after a
  pose. `visibility` and `lit` are not among them: both follow the frame that
  draws them, as `specs/sensing.md` fixes.
- A `reset` restores the fields above to their title-screen values and reseeds
  the game's random generator, leaving the game indistinguishable from one
  freshly started, so anything else the build keeps across frames follows from
  what a reset restores.
